WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:06.038
CASE and FILTER can express similar conditional
aggregation logic CASE

00:00:06.065 --> 00:00:11.144
is generally more portable because many SQL systems
support conditional expressions inside

00:00:11.170 --> 00:00:17.145
aggregates FILTER is concise and readable in
PostgreSQL especially

00:00:17.158 --> 00:00:22.252
when several measures each have their own condition
For this course you

00:00:22.279 --> 00:00:27.097
should understand both If you know the query will
run in PostgreSQL

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:31.229
FILTER can be a clean choice If you expect the SQL
to

00:00:31.255 --> 00:00:37.292
move between different platforms SUM with CASE is
often safer Whichever

00:00:37.319 --> 00:00:42.558
style you choose validate the measure using a small
known example Conditional

00:00:42.584 --> 00:00:46.611
metrics can look correct while using slightly
different conditions so checking a

00:00:46.624 --> 00:00:48.755
few rows manually is still important
